.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OPENSSL-QLOG 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openssl-qlog
.Nd OpenSSL qlog tracing functionality
.Sh DESCRIPTION
OpenSSL has unstable support for generating logs in the qlog logging
format, which can be used to obtain diagnostic data for QUIC
connections.
The data generated includes information on packets sent and received and
the frames contained within them, as well as loss detection and other
events.
.Pp
The qlog output generated by OpenSSL can be used to obtain diagnostic
visualisations of a given QUIC connection using tools such as
.Sy qvis .
.Pp
.Sy WARNING:
The output of OpenSSL's qlog functionality uses an unstable format based
on a draft specification.
qlog output is not subject to any format stability or compatibility
guarantees at this time, and
.Sy will
change in incompatible ways in future versions of OpenSSL. See
.Sy FORMAT STABILITY
below for details.
.Sh USAGE
When OpenSSL is built with qlog support, qlog is enabled at run time by
setting the standard
.Sy QLOGDIR
environment variable to point to a directory where qlog files should be
written.
Once set, any QUIC connection established by OpenSSL will have a qlog
file written automatically to the specified directory.
.Pp
Log files are generated in the
.Em .sqlog
format based on JSON-SEQ (RFC 7464).
.Pp
The filenames of generated log files under the specified
.Sy QLOGDIR
use the following structure:
.Bd -literal
    {connection_odcid}_{vantage_point_type}.sqlog
.Ed
.Pp
where
.Sy {connection_odcid}
is the lowercase hexadecimal encoding of a QUIC connection's Original
Destination Connection ID, which is the Destination Connection ID used
in the header of the first Initial packet sent as part of the connection
process, and
.Sy {vantage_point_type}
is either
.Qo Li client Qc
or
.Qo Li server Qc ,
reflecting the perspective of the endpoint producing the qlog output.
.Pp
The qlog functionality can be disabled at OpenSSL build time using the
.Em no-unstable-qlog
configure flag.
.Sh SUPPORTED EVENT TYPES
The following event types are currently supported:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy connectivity:connection_started
.It Sy connectivity:connection_state_updated
.It Sy connectivity:connection_closed
.It Sy transport:parameters_set
.It Sy transport:packet_sent
.It Sy transport:packet_received
.It Sy recovery:packet_lost
.El
.Sh FILTERS
By default, all supported event types are logged.
The
.Sy OSSL_QFILTER
environment variable can be used to configure a filter specification
which determines which event types are to be logged.
Each event type can be turned on and off individually.
The filter specification is a space-separated list of terms listing
event types to enable or disable.
The terms are applied in order, thus the effects of later terms override
the effects of earlier terms.
.Ss Examples
Here are some example filter specifications:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Qo Li * Qc No (or Qo Li +* Qc )
Enable all supported qlog event types.
.It Qo Li -* Qc
Disable all qlog event types.
.It Qo Li * -transport:packet_received Qc
Enable all qlog event types, but disable the
.Sy transport:packet_received
event type.
.It Qo Li -* transport:packet_sent Qc
Disable all qlog event types, except for the
.Sy transport:packet_sent
event type.
.It Qo Li -* connectivity:* transport:parameters_set Qc
Disable all qlog event types, except for
.Sy transport:parameters_set
and all supported event types in the
.Sy connectivity
category.
.El
.Ss Filter Syntax Specification
Formally, the format of the filter specification in ABNF is as follows:
.Bd -literal
    filter              = *filter-term

    filter-term         = add-sub-term

    add-sub-term        = ["-" / "+"] specifier

    specifier           = global-specifier / qualified-specifier

    global-specifier    = wildcard

    qualified-specifier = component-specifier ":" component-specifier

    component-specifier = name / wildcard

    wildcard            = "*"

    name                = 1*(ALPHA / DIGIT / "_" / "-")
.Ed
.Pp
Filter terms are interpreted as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Qo Li +* Qc No (or Qo Li * Qc )
Enables all event types.
.It Qo Li -* Qc
Disables all event types.
.It Qo Li +foo:* Qc No (or Qo Li foo:* Qc )
Enables all event types in the
.Sy foo
category.
.It Qo Li -foo:* Qc
Disables all event types in the
.Sy foo
category.
.It Qo Li +foo:bar Qc No (or Qo Li foo:bar Qc )
Enables a specific event type
.Sy foo:bar .
.It Qo Li -foo:bar Qc
Disables a specific event type
.Sy foo:bar .
.El
.Pp
Partial wildcard matches are not supported at this time.
.Ss Default Configuration
If the
.Sy OSSL_QFILTER
environment variable is not set or set to the empty string, this is
equivalent to enabling all event types (i.e., it is equivalent to a
filter of
.Qo Li * Qc Ns ).
Note that the
.Sy QLOGDIR
environment variable must also be set to enable qlog.
.Sh FORMAT STABILITY
The OpenSSL qlog functionality currently implements a draft version of
the qlog specification.
Future revisions to the qlog specification in advance of formal
standardisation are expected to introduce incompatible and breaking
changes to the qlog format.
The OpenSSL qlog functionality will transition to producing output in
this format in the future once standardisation is complete.
.Pp
Because of this, the qlog output of OpenSSL
.Sy will
change in incompatible and breaking ways in the future, including in
non-major releases of OpenSSL. The qlog output of OpenSSL is considered
unstable and not subject to any format stability or compatibility
guarantees at this time.
.Pp
Users of the OpenSSL qlog functionality must be aware that the output
may change arbitrarily between releases and that the preservation of
compatibility with any given tool between releases is not guaranteed.
.Ss Aims
The OpenSSL draft qlog functionality is primarily intended for use in
conjunction with the qvis tool
.Lk https://qvis.quictools.info/ .
In terms of format compatibility, the output format of the OpenSSL qlog
functionality is expected to track what is supported by qvis.
As such, future changes to the output of the OpenSSL qlog functionality
are expected to track changes in qvis as they occur, and reflect the
versions of qlog currently supported by qvis.
.Pp
This means that prior to the finalisation of the qlog standard, in the
event of a disparity between the current draft and what qvis supports,
the OpenSSL qlog functionality will generally aim for qvis compatibility
over compliance with the latest draft.
.Pp
As such, OpenSSL's qlog functionality currently implements qlog version
0.3 as defined in
.Sy draft-ietf-quic-qlog-main-schema-05
and
.Sy draft-ietf-quic-qlog-quic-events-04 .
These revisions are intentionally used instead of more recent revisions
due to their qvis compatibility.
.Sh LIMITATIONS
The OpenSSL implementation of qlog currently has the following
limitations:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Not all event types defined by the draft specification are implemented.
.It
Only the JSON-SEQ
.Pf ( Sy .sqlog )
output format is supported.
.It
Only the
.Sy QLOGDIR
environment variable is supported for configuring the qlog output
directory.
The standard
.Sy QLOGFILE
environment variable is not supported.
.It
There is no API for programmatically enabling or controlling the qlog
functionality.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl-quic 7 ,
.Xr openssl-env 7
